# Added during adversarial review (2026-08-04): the README/FINDINGS claim that
# "sve2_gemm.c compiles cleanly cross-compiled for -march=armv9.2-a+sve2+i8mm+bf16"
# had no persisted artifact backing it in results/, and this Mac has no real
# aarch64-linux-gnu sysroot (no glibc headers), so a normal `-target
# aarch64-linux-gnu` cross-build fails on `#include <stdlib.h>` before it ever
# gets to the SVE2 code. This log is a real, reproducible workaround: Apple
# clang's *own* ACLE intrinsic headers (arm_sve.h, from
# `$(clang -print-resource-dir)/include`) are target-independent, so pairing
# `-nostdinc` + `-isystem <that dir>` + three minimal shim headers
# (stdint.h/stdlib.h/string.h, just enough typedefs/prototypes to satisfy this
# one file, no libc bodies) with `-target aarch64-linux-gnu` lets clang cross-
# compile sve2_gemm.c for real, all the way to a genuine ELF aarch64 object
# with real SVE2/i8mm instructions in it (confirmed below) -- not merely a
# syntax check. This still does NOT substitute for running on real SVE2
# hardware (see the correctness suite's [skip] on this file, and the Spark CI
# lane) -- it only substantiates the narrower "compiles cleanly" claim.
$ clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu -march=armv9.2-a+sve2+i8mm+bf16 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -c sve2_gemm.c -o sve2_gemm.o
exit=0 (0 = clean compile, zero warnings, zero errors)

$ file sve2_gemm.o
/tmp/sve2_gemm_check.o: ELF 64-bit LSB relocatable, ARM aarch64, version 1 (SYSV), not stripped

$ objdump -d sve2_gemm.o | grep -E "smmla|fmad|whilelt" (confirms real SVE2/i8mm instructions were emitted, not a stub)
      90: 65a18440     	fmad	z0.s, p1/m, z2.s, z1.s
     1f4: 45019802     	smmla	z2.s, z0.b, z1.b
